OR MOLECULAR MEDICINE,</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>SUGIHARA, TAKASHI</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>WADHWA, RENU</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>YOSHIDA, AKIKO</name></addressbook></applicant></applicants><inventors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>SUGIHARA TAKASHI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>WADHWA RENU</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>YOSHIDA AKIKO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>SUGIHARA, TAKASHI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>WADHWA, RENU</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>YOSHIDA, AKIKO</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>Full-length cDNAs, which encode novel proteins (Gros1-L and Gros1-S) participating in the regulation of cell proliferation, have been successfully isolated from a human testis cDNA library. Also, full-length cDNAs encoding mouse homologs (mouse Gros1-L and Gros1-S) of human Gros1 have been isolated. Cells showing the exogenous expression of Gros1-L show a significantly decreased colony formation ratio, while cells showing the expression of Gros1 antisense RNA show a significantly increased colony formation ratio.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>La présente invention concerne des ADNc pleine longueur codant de nouvelles protéines (Gros1-L et Gros1-S) qui participent à la régulation de la prolifération cellulaire, isolés avec succès à partir d'une bibliothèque d'ADNc de testicules de souris. De plus, des ADNc pleine longueur codant des homologues de souris (Gros1-L et Gros1-S de souris) de Gros1 humains ont été isolés. Les cellules montrant l'expression exogène de Gros1-L présentent un taux de formation de colonies très réduit, alors que les cellules montrant l'expression d'ARN antisens Gros1 présentent un taux de formation de colonies fortement augmenté.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>